<script module>
  import benchServer from "$server/rpc/benchServer"

  // Format a nanosecond mean into the tightest readable unit — mirrors the CLI bench's `fmtNs`.
  function fmtNs(ns) {
    if (ns >= 1e6) return (ns / 1e6).toFixed(2) + " ms"
    if (ns >= 1e3) return (ns / 1e3).toFixed(2) + " µs"
    return ns.toFixed(0) + " ns"
  }

  // abide ÷ vanilla. A `†` marks a bench measured close to the timing loop's own cost, where the ratio
  // understates the real multiple (the same footnote the CLI bench prints).
  function fmtRatio(row) {
    if (row.ratio === null) return "—"
    return row.ratio.toFixed(2) + "×" + (row.nearFloor ? " †" : "")
  }
</script>

<main class="page">
  <h1>Server dispatch bench (live)</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    The other half of every request the <a href="/platform/bench">frontend bench</a> doesn't touch: the
    in-process per-request / per-read primitives on abide's hot path — route classification
    (<code>matchRoute</code>), cache-key building (<code>canonicalKey</code>), and the <code>memo</code>
    read + cache-verb surface. This runs the same <code>@abide/bench/serverBenches</code> recipes the CLI
    <code>bun run bench:server</code> measures, timed with the same loop, streamed one row per bench via
    <code>jsonl</code> so the table fills live via <code>&#123;#for await&#125;</code>. Refresh to re-run.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    Each recipe is timed <strong>twice</strong>: once through abide, once through the same work written by
    hand with no framework (a precompiled <code>RegExp</code> table instead of the router, a
    <code>JSON.stringify</code> key into a <code>Map</code> instead of the memo). The <strong>×</strong>
    column is abide ÷ vanilla — what the framework costs over doing it yourself, and the one figure here
    that does not depend on this machine.
  </p>

  <p class="lead">
    These are the tight, isolated numbers the framework's request path is made of — no loopback socket in
    the mix. The end-to-end <code>createTestApp</code> dispatch numbers live in the CLI runner only
    (<code>bun run bench:server</code>): booting a real server per page-load is the wrong shape for a live
    page.
  </p>

  <button
    data-testid="rerun"
    class="mb-4 rounded bg-slate-800 px-3 py-1.5 text-white"
    onclick={() => benchServer.refresh()}
  >
    Re-run bench
  </button>

  <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm" data-testid="bench-table">
    <thead>
      <tr class="border-b border-slate-300 text-left">
        <th class="py-1.5 pr-4">bench</th>
        <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right">ns/op</th>
        <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right">vanilla JS</th>
        <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right">×</th>
        <th class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right">iters</th>
        <th class="py-1.5">note</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {#for await row of benchServer()}
        <tr class="border-b border-slate-100" data-testid="bench-row">
          <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 font-mono">{row.group}/{row.name}</td>
          <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right font-mono">{fmtNs(row.nsPerOp)}</td>
          <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right font-mono text-slate-500">{row.vanillaNsPerOp === null ? "—" : fmtNs(row.vanillaNsPerOp)}</td>
          <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right font-mono font-semibold">{fmtRatio(row)}</td>
          <td class="py-1.5 pr-4 text-right font-mono text-slate-400">{row.iters}</td>
          <td class="py-1.5 font-mono text-slate-500">{row.vanillaNote === null ? row.note : row.note + " · vs " + row.vanillaNote}</td>
        </tr>
      {:catch}
        <tr><td colspan="6" class="py-2 text-red-600">bench failed</td></tr>
      {/for}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-500">
    † the op is close enough to the timing loop's own per-iteration cost that the constant — added to both
    sides — squashes the ratio toward 1.00×; the real multiple is higher.
  </p>
</main>
